statement:
The supplied sources do not contain a derivation of partial fractions, as partial fraction decomposition is an algebraic operation and representation definition rather than a derived result. In algebra, the partial fraction decomposition (or partial fraction expansion) of a rational fraction (a fraction such that the numerator and denominator are both polynomials) is an operation that consists of expressing the fraction as a sum of a polynomial (possibly zero) and one or several fractions with simpler denominators. For a proper rational function P(x)/Q(x) with \deg(P) < \deg(Q) where Q(x) = \prod_{j=1}^r (x - \lambda_j)^{
u_j}, the defining representation is P(x)/Q(x) = \sum_{j=1}^r A_j(x)/(x - \lambda_j)^{
u_j} subject to \deg(A_j) < 
u_j for each j.

